  "worked_solution": "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)=t-1,\\,z(t)={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & =i+j+{e}^{t}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1)\\cdot (1)+t\\cdot (1)+{e}^{t}\\cdot ({e}^{t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t}+2t-1\\,dt \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}+{t}^{2}-t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}+1-1)-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
